WEBVTT

00:00.250 --> 00:05.650
As a former IELTS examiner, I know how much clearer
an answer is when it follows a plan.

00:06.350 --> 00:07.860
So let's plan one together.

00:08.750 --> 00:12.040
Here's the museum graph,
and I'm starting a two-minute timer.

00:12.040 --> 00:13.000
Now.

00:14.100 --> 00:16.950
First, the overview. What's the big picture?

00:17.560 --> 00:22.740
All three museums got more visitors.
And the British Museum was far ahead the whole time.

00:23.580 --> 00:28.250
In my notes I write just five words:
all up, British Museum far ahead.

00:29.010 --> 00:34.600
Next, the grouping. The British Museum stands apart,
so it gets its own paragraph.

00:35.040 --> 00:38.900
The Tate and the Science Museum
go together in the second one.

00:39.820 --> 00:45.000
Now the figures. Paragraph one: the British Museum,

00:45.000 --> 00:48.510
2 million visitors in 1990
and 6 million in 2020.

00:49.960 --> 00:51.520
That's three times as many.

00:52.330 --> 00:56.200
Paragraph two: the Tate, from 0.8 million to 3 million.

00:56.200 --> 01:00.700
The Science Museum went up and down,
but finished at 2.5 million.

01:01.940 --> 01:02.700
And stop.

01:03.090 --> 01:08.630
That's the whole plan: an overview idea, a grouping
and a few figures for each paragraph.

01:09.310 --> 01:11.600
Not sentences.

01:12.380 --> 01:13.200
Notes.

01:13.750 --> 01:17.720
Now it's your turn.
Plan the graph on the right page in two minutes.
